            <ArticleTitle>Rapid communication: laparoscopic Anderson-Hynes dismembered pyeloplasty using the da Vinci robot: technical considerations.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>79-83</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To present our initial experience with laparoscopic pyeloplasty utilizing the da Vinci robot for upper tract reconstruction.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE REPORT" NlmCategory="METHODS">A four-port transperitoneal approach was used in a 73-year-old man. The ureteropelvic (UPJ) obstruction was identified with a crossing vessel. After dismemberment of the UPJ, the renal pelvis was trimmed and reconstructed using the da Vinci robot. The total operative time was 5 hours; the time spent for reconstruction was 45 minutes. Blood loss was &lt;150 mL. The postoperative analgesic requirement was 8 mg of morphine and 25 mg of hydrocodone. There were no intraoperative or postoperative complications.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The da Vinci robot can serve as a vital surgical tool during pyeloplasty with extensive reconstruction.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
